  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nMonitoring pattern trends for 20-40% rain chances this weekend:\n\nThe ridge forecast to be over the region Thursday and Friday is\nprogged to be quite amplified/meridional. Global ensemble\nmembers are depicting a low-amplitude shortwave trough to \nprogress through the west side of this ridge Friday and \nSaturday. Due to the amplified, but thin and meridional nature \nof the ridge paired with the low amplitude of the wave, it seems\nmodel members are struggling a bit on timing and placement of \nthe shortwave trough. This is leading to uncertainty in the \nprogression of a surface warm front and attendant rain chances \ninto our area. \n\nIt seems there are two main scenarios. The first is the\nshortwave breaks the ridge down, and the wave rounds the top of\nthe ridge and drops through the central CONUS. This scenario\nwould bring a higher chance of rain to our area. The other\nscenario, is that instead of fully breaking down the ridge, it\nshifts the axis to be oriented northeastward, and the wave lifts\noff into the Great Lakes. This scenario would bring lower rain\nchances to our area. So, for now, chances are 20-40% across the\narea given the uncertainty in placement and timing. The pattern\nwill continue to be monitored for any preference of the two\nscenarios. Either way, any chances of severe weather is very \nlow as appreciable shear should stay well north of the area. It \nwill mainly come down to coverage and longevity of any rain \nchances.\n\nTemperatures should warm, though, as the ridge traverses the\narea, with forecast highs bumping up into the 80s for Saturday\nand Sunday. There is then even more uncertainty in the pattern\ngoing into next week, bringing wildly large temperature spreads.\nThis is largely due to the transition to fall weather with the\njet stream becoming a bit more variable, promoting chances for\ncold fronts to move through the area. While the 25th-75th \npercentile temperature spreads hover within the 80s to near 90 \n(consolidating greater confidence to that temperature range), \nthe tails spread all the way from near records in the mid-90s, \nto somewhat cooler fall weather near 70 F. We will also continue\nto monitor these trends for any changes in the mean forecast\ntemperatures next week.",
